    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "No, you're absolutely right. And so when I think of the role model of leadership, it goes to my dad who served in World War II, United States Army of Corporal. And the interesting thing is that he served at a time when the Army was still segregated. And for him to go and fight for this nation and, you know, raise his right hand to take that oath to support and defend the Constitution and met the world to me. And so, you know, watching my dad and how he led our home and and all the things that he did to invest in our education and our growth as young men. And I remember at the age of 15, my dad set me down on the steps of our home there, 651, Kennesaw Avenue, Northeast Atlanta, Georgia, 30308, 404, 8742836. And it was located in the historical fourth war neighborhood that produced Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr. And he told me that there is no greater honor than to wear the uniform in the United States of America. that he wanted me to be the first officer in our family because he was an enlisted man of Corporal. My older brother was an enlisted Marine Lance Corporal in Vietnam. My mom did a little over 25 years civilian service with the six Marine Corps District headquarters in Atlanta. So I stepped up and I took that challenge and I think that's one of the great things about leadership is that it's not about you, it's not about what you accomplish or achieve, it's about what you enable others to accomplish and to achieve. And you got to set some high standards out there, but you got to enable people to be able to achieve and attain those standards. And so for me that day 31 July 1982 at the University of Tennessee, Stokely Athletic Center where mom and dad were on either shoulder and They pinned those gold bars of an army second lieutenant on my shoulders. That was just a very incredible and emotional day for us. And that was the only time I ever saw my dad cry.",
