  {
    "episode_id": 51681,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think it was that call. And I remember my dad telling me about how he was sitting there in South Georgia, Cuthbert, Georgia, Randolph County. And he heard the news come over the radio about the Japanese bombing Pearl Harbor. And at that time, no one cared about skin color. He was an American. And he knew just the same as my godfather, who was a Tuskegee Airmen, they knew that the country needed them. And so they were willing to step up. And I think it was so important that that generation didn't sit back and they didn't ask for reparations. They didn't act like they were victims. They said that we all are part of this thing called the United States of America. We've been attacked and our country needs us now. And so that same mentality was what he transferred to each and every one of us so that, you know, I end up being a 22 year career army officer, Lieutenant Colonel retired. And now his grandson, my nephew, who cares his namesake, Herman West III, is a Lieutenant Colonel in the United States Army. And he will be commanding an artillery battalion next year. He's currently stationed out at Fort Lewis, Washington. And just this past summer, my youngest daughter's husband, he completed the Army basic training and also his advanced individual training. And he is a junior at the University of North Texas, working toward his commission in the United States Army. So is that incredible legacy of service, sacrifice and commitment that we see? And I think that's part of late leadership. And people always say, you know, what was your family business? And I say serving the country was our family business. That's right.",
