    "turn_text": "Yeah, because he wanted me, you know, my dad had a saying, you know, find out what the standard is and exceed it. And so he did not just want his son to go in and look, I started high school junior ROTC back in 1976. I mean, like I said, I was 15. And so I was on that path to go on to the University of Tennessee in the Army ROTC program. So I think that my dad wanted a little bit more and that's how we challenged subsequent generations. But I'll never forget on that day back in October of 1983, when I was getting ready to head out and go to my first duty station, which was Fort Sill, Oklahoma, because I was an artillery officer. And my dad and my older brother, they sat me down and dad looked at me out of eye and he had that serious look. And he said, I want you to understand something, son, you may be a lieutenant. But you've got to understand the most important thing is you got to take care of your soldiers. And that just always throughout my 22 years in the military, it just reverberated that I got to take care of my soldiers. That's the number one important thing about being an officer is not about the rank. And I, through the years, I would always try to get young officers to understand is that your soldiers are going to respect you because they have to. You're in uniform and they have to salute you, call you sir or ma'am or whatever. But if they really respect you, If they see you, you know, offside off the duty station and civilian clothes and they still come up to you and refer to you as serve ma'am. That's how you know that they respect you and you got to honor that respect and you got to continue to serve them being in uniform or out of uniform.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
