  {
    "episode_id": 51681,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "I got to tell you, when I was a cadet and we had our officers training camp out at Fort Lewis, Washington, and you're right, all of the different branches come out and give you their best displays and everything. And I remember seeing the artillery, you know, we're sitting in the bleachers and the artillery comes in and they actually had the howitzer on a sling load underneath a CH-47 helicopter. And I'm like, this is so cool. And so they bring it in and of course the prop blast is blowing us, you know, in the stands. They set the howitzer down and then the crew repels out of the helicopter on ropes. And then they come down and they, you know, they haven't played on the loud screen. They get a fire mission. They tell them what the grid is and they start cranking that sucker, putting it into action. They set the quadrant. They set the deflection and boom, they put that round down range. I was sold. I want to jump out of helicopters with that. I want to shoot these big things and go boom. Because I would say, man, the infantry is pretty cool, you know, and get out there and be like my older brother who's in Vietnam. Nah, nothing like shooting those big guns, man. And so I was a paratrooper. I'm a master rated Army paratrooper and also Navy Marine Corps, paratroopers insignia and the Army Air Assault, helicopter assault, wings as well, and the Italian paratrooper wings. So I just fell in love with it at that moment and said, this is something I want. And the other thing was that I've always liked math and physics. And back in the day, you had to learn the mechanics of artillery. It wasn't about a computer. You had to use tabular tables and slide rules and all of these things to figure out how you take this incredibly big bullet that can be 55, 60 piles. And you could send it from this point, 10, 15 kilometers. And you got to be within that 50 meters of that target or 30 meters of that target. That's what I want to do.",
