    "turn_text": "that. And that, you know, they always gave us introductory courses on, of course, how this worked and generally understanding how to call for fire at those kinds of things. But when you're on the other side of that weapon pace, just the whole different world that I started to grow an appreciation for. When you talk about the airborne, this is actually a great segue. So you read my mind. I grew up as well as my first job, you know, jumping out airplanes, being a business leader, jumping. It's a different mentality. I'll tell you when I was at the academy, one of my mentors out there, I was picking jobs and I'm from Texas. So I was going to pick a job to go back here, Fort Hood, do something here in Texas. There's no airborne units out here, as you know. And my mentor at the time, my tactical officer had told me, he says, you know, just what is a great choice. Great units out there. First cab is a great unit. No matter what about it. He goes, but look, if you really want to sit your career right and just really understand the origin of leadership, this is joining a unit with an Airborne tab over it. And I was like, what do you mean? He goes, just trust me. Can you talk a little bit about your experience leading soldiers in Airborne Union? How that's a little bit more intense, I would say, or brings a different dynamic.",
