  {
    "episode_id": 51681,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "what you don't forget, right? You don't forget a little things like that. And so being there as that, you know, you become a stick leader. And in the airborne, the officer leads. I mean, you're the guy that jumps out. You're the jump master and all these things. And so that was an very important thing for me to understand and learn is that you lead from the front. You lead by example. And so here I was in week number three at airborne school and that very first job and I'm the first guy to go. And you know, they opened up the door and you know that sound. It's a sound unlike anything else of an aircraft going 125 miles per hour. And you look down, you know, 800 to 1000 feet. And you're thinking, I'm really about to do this. I'm really about to put my life, you know, trust this parachute and this rigor who I don't know. But then you look behind me behind you and you see 63 of the troops. And you know that they're looking at you. Because if they see fear, they see hesitation or whatever, that's something that could, you know, sift on down. So when the green light came on, I was gone. No one was going to say that Lieutenant West, you know, froze in the door or hesitated in the door. I was going to be that leader. And that is the model of an airborne leader. You know, when you go to Fort Bragg and you see that incredible statue of Iron Mike, I mean, that's what it means to be a paratrooper. And it means that you take care of your men first, you eat last, you make sure that they're bunked down, you make sure that they're equipped, they're trained, they're ready, they have enough ammunition. So my first tour duty was in Vicenza, Italy with the airborne battalion combat team. There could not have been a better assignment for me to start off as a lieutenant and to get the opportunity to work with other NATO member airborne battalions and talk to their officers and get their lessons learned. It set the course for me for the rest of my career.",
