  {
    "episode_id": 51681,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "the little, the little black Chinook will come and pick you up and you were gone because he was serious about officers have to, you know, maintain and hold a standard. So was that guy then. Lieutenant Colonel McNeil, who later on became a four-star general, General Needham became a two-star general. And then, of course, my first combat tour duty, Operation Desert Storm, had an incredible battalion commander, Lieutenant Colonel John Gingrich, who was a Texas A&M, an Aggie, graduate and packable man. But I think the guy that had the greatest impact on me was Colonel Denny R. Lewis. He was the brigade commander of the 18th field artillery brigade airborne at Fort Bragg. And I was his brigade S3 and later on became battalion exo under him. And all of the things that he taught us, and I remember that there were times he just take the officers out on an officer run, and only he and I would know the course. And it was interesting how he would mentally get into the head of a lot of the young officers, figuring, okay, we're just gonna turn around here and turn around there. But it was all about building your mental dexterity and your mental toughness. And the motto that he gave us was tough, proud, and disciplined. And so that's something that I will say for any leader, business, military, whatever. You got to be tough to make those hard decisions. You got to be proud in your organization. You got to be disciplined in all that you say that you do and do.",
