  {
    "episode_id": 51681,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 59,
    "turn_text": "Well, you got some folks that you know this very well, Joseph, that they can be really good Garrison leaders. They know the manual and they can quote, you know, rule and regulation, whatever. But when a bullet starts flying, they just lock up and they're not ready to go out there and lead. So I thought it was very important for me to have that balance to be able to understand what it meant to be. an artilleryman and understand the principles of artillery, but also understand the principles of leadership as well. And so when I look at Desert Seal Desert Storm and compare it to Iraq and also Afghanistan, you know, wide open Desert Warfare, that's what we were good at. That's why we went out to the National Training Center. And so, you know, really, Desert Seal Desert Storm was kind of like a training rotation at the National Training Center said we had live bullets and live targets. You",
