    "turn_idx": 71,
    "turn_text": "is a failure and a father of leadership. You know, one of the worst things I remember someone saying to me, and I'm trying to remember if I was a Brigade X or whatever, But when a commander looks at you and says, you know, I really don't know what I want. So just write something up and bring it to me. That's horrible. That's like shooting in the dark. And so you as a leader, your responsibility is to give people the left and right limits. Like I said, the task and purpose. And I was always looking for it. I used to call them the fire and forget weapon system type of subordinate leader. that, you know, all I had to do was pull the trigger and I knew that they hit the target because, you know, they knew exactly the asthma that they had to be on. And so I think it's important that you sit down and you have those, you know, professional development sessions with your team and you talk about things or maybe you have the, you know, we used to have the hot wash AARs and if something happens, You know, get some butcher paper pros and cons, think about what just happened and capture that because you got to learn from those experiences. But, you know, when I was going and visiting small businesses when I was a member of Congress, because I was on the small business committee, I would always ask the small businesses in the congressional district I represented. When was the last time you sat down and talked with your employees? When was the last time you told them, you know, the decisions that you had to make in this past week or the past two weeks? So they understood. When was the last time you told them that these were some of the upcoming decisions that you were facing and how would it affect them? And so the biggest part about being an effective leader is being an effective communicator.",
