    "turn_text": "Well, I would tell you something that I used to do as a brigade operations officer, an exo, and a battalion commander. You know, the artillery, like I said, when I came in, you had the slide rules, the tabular firing tables, you had to figure out, you know, how to get that data. And you still had to maintain those time standards. Well, everyone knew at some point in time of us being on the field, the Major West or Lieutenant Corps West would say, all of this digital electronic stuff is dead. You got to go back old school. Now, it would just absolutely freak the young officers out because they're just sitting around wondering, is he going to do it today? Is he going to do it tomorrow? And the sergeists were just like, I love it. I can't wait for him to do it. And so you've got to train for those times. You know, I remember the day when we were out there at the National Training Center, you had a map and a compass. You had to understand Polaris co-champ,",
