    "turn_text": "the stars, you know, to get around. You didn't just plug a grid coordinate into, you know, a GPS thing. And it says, here's your waypoints. You turn here. You don't know. I mean, you could get, you know, freaking lost. And that was one of the things that you want to avoid being a lieutenant, being the dumb lieutenant that got his unit lost. And so I think it's so important that we train people for those scenarios when some part of your resources or whatever, is not there, is not available. So how do we continue to operate? And so I always get people to understand you've got to prepare for that worst case scenario. You got to think about that. You got to be ready for it. And guess what? Maybe you should stop and train on this and allow that to happen and be able to have that rapid response. And everyone has to realize this. One of the things that I think so many people don't understand is that What happens when Joe or Jane get sick and they can't come in or they're going for a month or something happens and they're going period, you know, the operation has to continue on. So I used to always say that you have to be the expert in your lane and knowledgeable in another. So that, you know, when you look at our artillery crew of eight or nine soldiers, if the number one man goes down, someone is stepping up in there to be able to do the number one man duties and responsibilities on that house. So I think those are the type of things that we have to, you know, continue to do. And you got to stay sharp. You got to always continue to think through this thing, because that's the type of scenario that we're in. With today's modern technology, things change within the twinkling of an eye. You got to be prepared for that.",
