    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 87,
    "turn_text": "You know, we had to say, sweat saves blood. That was our motto of the 18 field artillery brigade airborne for Brad. And so I understand that the sweat that you do in training, it will save the blood that you could lose when it's the real thing. And so I think it's so important that you got to maintain those those training peers, those leader development peers, those employee development sessions, and take the time. I don't care if it's 30 minutes. I don't care if it's an hour, a week, to be able to unplug and have a dedicated time. Remember, back in the day, we used to have that thing called Sergeant's Time Training, where every Thursday, You know, officers, you know, you go away. We do the officer thing. We have our professional development, but the NCOs and non-commissioned officers are working with those soldiers on those basic skills and tasks. And, you know, it was sacrosanct. I mean, if you were an officer and you tried to monkey around with Sergeant's time training, oh, man, you're going to see the division commander.",
