  {
    "episode_id": 51681,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 93,
    "turn_text": "You know, I would say the great thing about being up there as a member of Congress was the ability to be able to serve others. When you are there in the grocery store, back in your local home area, and someone comes up to you and says, hey, Congressman, I'm having this problem. Can you help me out? Here's my car. Give me your information. And you get them assistance. If you have a veteran that comes up and says, a lot of my military wars or records, I've lost them. And you can get them their awards. And you have that ceremony where you pin the back on. That just warms your heart. When you can wake up in the morning, and you can go for a PT run, and you go past the United States Capitol in the early dawn light, especially on a foggy day, you see that beautiful structure, and you think about how once upon a time, I was deployed to protect this institution. And now this is where I call my office, this is where I work. That's the American dream. But the thing that was just really gut wrenching for me was when people are talking to you and they know that they're lying to you, you know that they're lying to you and even they know that you know that they're lying to you and they still do it. That was the thing that really unnerved me the most because, you know, I told him my yes, yes, my no is a no. I'm a team player, but the thing that I'm going to always honor is the respect and regard of my constituents and the respect and regard and the duty I have to support and defend the Constitution. That's it. And so it was real tough. You know, to see people that were willing to, you know, just just go along to get along and not make that stand, you know, my mom taught me early on that a man must stand for something else or fall for anything. And I think, again, that comes back to, you know, who we are in the military in that. You know, my favorite ancient military, you know, warriors with the Spartans. There was no surrender. There was no retreat. You think about Travis's letter from the Alamo where he said those words. I will not surrender. I will not retreat. So I think that As a leader, you've got to have the courage to stand up when it's necessary. You have to have the competence to know what you're standing up for. You have to have a commitment to those core sets of principles and values. But you also have a conviction that says, I will stick to it, no matter what the wins may be at the time. And then you have to have the character, as I say, to do what is right when no one's watching. So those are the five C's of leadership that the Army taught me. I'll carry with me wherever I go.",
