    "turn_idx": 116,
    "turn_text": "Communications is so important because people have to understand where your mind is, they have to understand your commanders, your leaders intent so that they can understand their task and purpose. But I remember as an evaluator of cadets when I taught ROTC and I went back out there to evaluate a platoon at Fort Lewis many years later, someone asked me about this leadership trait called compassion. And they said, well, Captain West, how do you look at compassion in a leader? I said, it's very simple. What's the standard? Because a compassionate leader always has to look at the standard. So if it is cold, it is wet, it is rainy, it's absolutely freaking miserable. But yet you have to have someone out there on guard shift, a guard duty. They have to be out there making sure they're secure in the perimeter. Now, what does a compassionate leader do? Does a compassionate leader say, you know, it really sucks out there. Maybe I should bring them in and let them stay in a nice warm tent and everything like that. No, because you just blew the standard. What a compassionate leader does is he goes out there and he stands watch with them. Or the compassionate leader makes sure that hot soup and coffee is rotating around and you're the guy that's going out there and taking it to them. You know, so I think it's so important in these days and times that there's so many leaders thinking that they're being compassionate when they're hurting their employees because they're bending to cultural whims and things that are temporary and coddling them. You know, look, you know, we didn't dial it in in the military. You know, hey, can I just work from home today? You know, I don't need to come in and go to the rifle range, you know, because that noise, it really unnerves me. No, there's a standard. And you got to achieve the standard. And oh, by the way, if you go to the rifle range and you still didn't qualify, we're going to stay out there until you do qualify. We're going to put you on the weaponier system, the virtual system, so you can get better at it. But we're not going to tell you, you're OK. And guess what? We'll still give you a marksmanship badge as well. There was a standard, you have to adhere to the standard. And compassion means that you're enabling people, as my dad say, not just to meet the standard, but to exceed it.",
